{"metadata": {"team_id": "grilllab", "run_id": "grilllab-larf-finetuned-rankllm", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "1-1_2", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "For managing acid reflux, focus on foods that are naturally low in fat and acidity, high in fiber, and gentle on the digestive system. Here’s a concise guide to the best options:\n\n**Top Foods for Acid Reflux Relief**\n\n- **Vegetables:** Broccoli, leafy greens, asparagus, cucumbers, green beans, Brussels sprouts, peas, and potatoes. These are low in fat and acidity, and their alkaline nature helps neutralize stomach acid.\n- **Whole Grains:** Oatmeal, brown rice, and whole grain breads. High fiber content helps absorb stomach acid and supports digestion.\n- **Lean Proteins:** Skinless chicken, turkey, fish (especially grilled, steamed, or boiled), seafood, tofu, and egg whites. Avoid frying or adding fatty sauces.\n- **Non-Citrus Fruits:** Bananas and melons are especially gentle and can soothe the esophagus. Avoid citrus fruits like oranges and lemons.\n- **Healthy Fats:** Small amounts of avocados, walnuts, flaxseed, and olive oil are preferable to saturated fats.\n- **Other Soothing Options:** Ginger (fresh or as tea), fat-free or low-fat yogurt, and unsweetened coconut water can help calm symptoms.\n\n**Foods to Avoid:** Fatty or fried foods, spicy dishes, citrus fruits, caffeine, garlic, and onions, as these can trigger or worsen reflux.\n\nWould you like meal ideas using these ingredients, or tips on how to prepare them to further minimize reflux symptoms?", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0044-72-05971:1": 1.0, "clueweb22-en0024-83-01996:17": 0.5, "clueweb22-en0021-74-19197:1": 0.3333333333333333, "clueweb22-en0039-65-00093:4": 0.25, "clueweb22-en0009-09-00824:2": 0.2}, "ptkb_provenance": ["I eat dinner late at night.", "I enjoy apple, kiwi, and watermelon.", "I want to increase my protein intake.", "I like a variety of fruits."]}], "references": {"clueweb22-en0044-72-05971:1": 1.0, "clueweb22-en0024-8