{"metadata": {"team_id": "grilllab", "run_id": "grilllab-larf-finetuned-rankllm", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "2-1_3", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "You’re looking for ways to make coffee taste better because you dislike the core flavor, not just the bitterness or additives. Here’s how to transform your coffee experience:\n\n**1. Soften or Mask the Coffee Flavor**\n- **Add Flavored Syrups or Extracts:** Vanilla, coconut, or caramel syrups can dramatically change the taste profile, making coffee sweeter and less “coffee-like.”\n- **Spices:** Cinnamon or cocoa powder can add warmth and complexity, shifting the flavor away from pure coffee.\n- **Creaminess:** Dairy (milk, half-and-half, creamer) or plant-based alternatives (oat, almond milk) mellow the taste and texture.\n\n**2. Change Your Brewing Method**\n- **Cold Brew:** This method produces a smoother, less acidic, and often sweeter cup, which many people who dislike traditional coffee enjoy.\n\n**3. Adjust Strength and Add Water**\n- **Brew Weaker Coffee:** Use more water or less coffee grounds to reduce intensity.\n- **Americano Style:** Add hot water to espresso or strong coffee for a milder flavor.\n\n**4. Reduce Bitterness and Acidity**\n- **Pinch of Salt or Baking Soda:** Both can neutralize bitterness and acidity, making the drink smoother.\n\n**5. Try Specialty Drinks**\n- **Caramel Macchiato or Similar:** These drinks use milk and flavorings to mask the coffee taste almost entirely.\n\n**Personalized Tip:** Since your issue is with the core coffee flavor, start with cold brew or a flavored latte using plenty of milk and a syrup you like.\n\nWould you like a simple recipe or guidance on choosing the right beans and syrups for your taste?", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0029-28-15775:7": 1.0, "clueweb22-en0029-28-15775:6": 0.5, "clueweb22-en0026-83-04609:17": 0.3333333333333333, "clueweb22-en0039-57-02591:6": 0.25, "clueweb22-en0007-67-02540:1": 0.2}, "ptkb_provenance": ["I occasionally experienc