{"metadata": {"team_id": "grilllab", "run_id": "grilllab-larf-finetuned-rankllm", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "2-1_5", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "Here’s a concise guide to coffee types and how they affect taste—especially if you dislike sourness:\n\n**Coffee Bean Types**\n- **Arabica:** Sweeter, smoother, often with fruity or berry notes. Light roasts of Arabica can taste sour or sharp, especially if you’re used to darker, richer flavors.\n- **Robusta:** Stronger, more bitter, less sweet. Tends to have a harsher, earthier taste and is less likely to be sour, but can be more bitter.\n\n**Roast Levels**\n- **Light Roast:** Retains more acidity and fruity flavors—often perceived as sour. Not ideal if you dislike sourness.\n- **Medium Roast:** Balanced flavor and aroma, less acidity than light roast. Good middle ground.\n- **Dark Roast:** Bold, strong, and more bitter. Sourness is minimized, but bitterness increases. If you dislike sourness, dark or medium-dark roasts are your best bet.\n\n**Other Factors**\n- **Freshness:** Stale beans can taste sharply sour or “off.” Always use fresh beans.\n- **Origin & Blends:** Beans from different regions or blends can have unique flavor profiles, but roast level and bean type are the biggest factors for sourness.\n\n**Recommendation for Less Sour Coffee**\n- Choose a medium or dark roast, preferably from Arabica beans for smoothness.\n- Avoid light roasts and ensure your beans are fresh.\n\nWould you like tips on specific brands or brewing methods to further reduce sourness?", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0011-03-12177:6": 1.0, "clueweb22-en0009-48-16939:4": 0.5, "clueweb22-en0003-82-03894:2": 0.3333333333333333, "clueweb22-en0022-18-11457:2": 0.25, "clueweb22-en0003-00-04486:10": 0.2}, "ptkb_provenance": ["I start my day by drinking coffee.", "I want to lose weight.", "I don't eat sugar."]}], "references": {"clueweb22-en0011-03-12177:6": 1.0, "clueweb22-en0009-48-16939:4": 0.5, "clueweb22-en0003-82-03894:2": 0.333