{"metadata": {"team_id": "grilllab", "run_id": "grilllab-larf-finetuned-rankllm", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "2-1_11", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "Roasting is the single most important factor shaping the taste, aroma, and overall character of your coffee—often even more than the bean variety itself.\n\n**How Roasting Impacts Coffee:**\n\n- **Flavor Development:** Roasting transforms green coffee beans by triggering chemical reactions (Maillard and caramelization) that create hundreds of new flavor and aroma compounds. The degree of roast (light, medium, dark) determines which flavors dominate.\n    - *Light roasts* preserve more of the bean’s original, often fruity or floral notes, and tend to be brighter and more acidic.\n    - *Medium roasts* balance acidity and body, offering a rounded, complex flavor.\n    - *Dark roasts* develop bold, smoky, and sometimes bitter or woody flavors, with lower acidity and more pronounced roast character—often masking subtle bean differences.\n\n- **Aroma:** Most of coffee’s aroma comes from volatile compounds formed during roasting. The longer and hotter the roast, the more these compounds evolve, sometimes degrading delicate aromas but enhancing deeper, richer notes.\n\n- **Body and Acidity:** Lighter roasts are lighter-bodied and more acidic. Darker roasts are fuller-bodied, smoother, and less acidic—often preferred by those who dislike sourness.\n\n- **Liberica & Roasting:** Since you’re interested in Liberica’s bold, woody profile and want to avoid sourness, a medium-dark to dark roast will highlight its signature richness and minimize acidity.\n\n**Recommendation:**  \nChoose a medium-dark or dark roast Liberica. This will give you a full-bodied, smooth cup with smoky, woody notes and little to no sourness.\n\nWould you like tips on brewing methods that best suit dark-roasted Liberica?", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0016-45-10462:1": 1.0, "clueweb22-en0016-45-10462:0": 0.5, "clueweb22-en0039-27-01568:2": 0.3333