{"metadata": {"team_id": "grilllab", "run_id": "grilllab-larf-finetuned-rankllm", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "3-1_1", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "You’re an experienced outdoor climber with finger pain. Here’s what matters most for you:\n\n**Most Likely Cause**\n- Finger pulley injuries (especially A2) are the most common in climbers. These often present as pain at the base of the finger, sometimes after a “pop,” and can involve swelling or difficulty bending/straightening the finger.\n\n**Immediate Steps**\n- Stop climbing and avoid stressing the finger further—continuing can worsen the injury.\n- If you heard a pop, see swelling, or have significant pain, consult a hand specialist or orthopedic doctor familiar with climbing injuries. Imaging (ultrasound/MRI) may be needed to assess severity.\n\n**Self-Management (if mild, no severe swelling or deformity)**\n- Begin gentle range-of-motion exercises: slowly bend and straighten the finger within pain-free limits, holding for a few seconds, several times a day.\n- Do not push through pain.\n- For moderate pain or partial tears, short-term immobilization (splinting) may help, followed by gradual movement.\n- Avoid climbing or hangboarding until pain-free and strength returns.\n\n**Prevention (for future)**\n- Warm up fingers thoroughly before climbing.\n- Use open-hand or half-crimp grips rather than full crimping.\n- Progress finger training (hangboarding, etc.) cautiously.\n\n**Why this matters for you:**  \nYour experience and outdoor focus mean you’re at higher risk for pulley injuries. Early, appropriate management is key to a full recovery and safe return to climbing.\n\nWould you like guidance on finding a climbing-savvy hand specialist, or more details on taping and rehab exercises?", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:1": 1.0, "clueweb22-en0021-66-06454:13": 0.5, "clueweb22-en0015-71-10781:0": 0.3333333333333333, "clueweb22-en0021-66-06454:12": 0.25, "clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:7": 0.2}, "