{"metadata": {"team_id": "grilllab", "run_id": "grilllab-larf-finetuned-rankllm", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "3-1_2", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "Your finger pain is likely related to your climbing, especially given your regular outdoor activity over the past 3 years. Here’s what matters most for climbers in your situation:\n\n**Most Common Cause:**\n- **Finger Pulley Injury**: The A2 pulley (at the base of the finger) is the most frequently injured structure in climbers. This injury often presents as pain (sometimes swelling) at the base of the finger, especially when bending or straightening it. A loud “pop” or visible bulging (“bowstringing”) signals a more severe injury.\n\n**Why This Happens:**\n- Repeated stress from gripping small holds, especially with crimp or half-crimp positions, puts strain on the finger pulleys and flexor tendons. Overuse and projecting harder routes increase risk.\n\n**What Climbers Like You Experience:**\n- Many regular climbers report recurring finger pain or minor injuries, particularly as they push into harder grades. Some adapt by using open-hand or half-crimp grips to reduce strain.\n\n**What To Do Now:**\n- **Rest**: Avoid climbing or loading the painful finger until pain subsides.\n- **Monitor**: If you notice swelling, a “pop,” or bulging, seek medical evaluation to rule out a severe tear.\n- **Grip Modification**: Favor open-hand grips over full crimping to reduce future risk.\n- **Rehabilitation**: Gradual return with finger strengthening and mobility exercises is key.\n\nWould you like specific rehab exercises, or guidance on when it’s safe to return to climbing?", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:1": 1.0, "clueweb22-en0017-96-08323:0": 0.5, "clueweb22-en0001-34-18428:18": 0.3333333333333333, "clueweb22-en0038-33-02567:13": 0.25, "clueweb22-en0008-84-09982:22": 0.2}, "ptkb_provenance": ["I am climbing regularly for the past 3 years.", "I climb outdoors."]}], "references": {"clueweb22-en0007-15-09796