{"metadata": {"team_id": "grilllab", "run_id": "grilllab-larf-finetuned-rankllm", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "3-1_3", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "Here’s a concise overview of finger anatomy and injury, tailored for climbers who aren’t sure where their pain is coming from:\n\n**Finger Anatomy (Climbing-Relevant)**\n- **Bones & Joints:** Each finger has three bones and three hinged joints.\n- **Tendons:** Flexor tendons run along the palm side, connecting forearm muscles to the finger bones—these allow you to bend your fingers and grip holds.\n- **Pulleys:** Tough bands of connective tissue (annular pulleys, labeled A1–A5) wrap around the tendons, holding them close to the bone. This prevents the tendon from “bowstringing” away from the bone during forceful gripping (like crimping).\n\n**Common Climbing Injury: Pulley Injury**\n- **What Happens:** Excessive force (especially during crimping) can damage or rupture these pulleys, most often the A2 at the base of the finger.\n- **Symptoms:** Sudden pain (sometimes with a “pop”), swelling, and pain when bending or straightening the finger. Severe cases may show visible bulging (“bowstringing”).\n- **Tendons vs. Pulleys:** While you may feel pain along the tendon, the underlying issue is often with the pulley system that supports it.\n\n**Why This Matters for You**\n- If your pain is at the base of the finger and worsens with gripping or bending, a pulley injury is likely.\n- Taping and rest are common first steps, but severe symptoms (like bowstringing or inability to bend) require medical attention.\n\nWould you like guidance on self-assessment, taping, or when to see a doctor?", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:1": 1.0, "clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:0": 0.5, "clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:2": 0.3333333333333333, "clueweb22-en0022-93-05597:0": 0.25, "clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:4": 0.2}, "ptkb_provenance": ["I am climbing regularly for the past 3 years.", "Many years ago I had a bad fall while bouldering.