{"metadata": {"team_id": "grilllab", "run_id": "grilllab-larf-finetuned-rankllm", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "3-1_4", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "Here’s how finger pain presents in different areas relevant to climbing injuries, so you can better localize your pain:\n\n**Pulley Injuries (especially A2)**\n- **Location:** Pain is usually at the base of the finger, on the palm side.\n- **Symptoms:** Sharp pain when bending or straightening the finger, sometimes accompanied by swelling. A loud “pop” at the time of injury is common. In severe cases, you may see “bowstringing”—a visible bulge when you try to bend the finger.\n- **Climbing Link:** Most common in climbers, especially after using a crimp grip or making a dynamic move to a small hold.\n\n**Flexor Tendon Injuries**\n- **Location:** Pain and tenderness along the palm side of the finger, sometimes extending into the palm.\n- **Symptoms:** Difficulty or inability to bend the finger, pain when trying to flex, and sometimes numbness at the fingertip. If the tendon is ruptured, you may not be able to bend the finger at all.\n- **Climbing Link:** Can occur with forceful grips or sudden pulls; less common than pulley injuries but more serious.\n\n**How to Differentiate:**\n- **Pulley:** Pain is focused at the base of the finger, especially with crimping or resisted bending.\n- **Tendon:** Pain runs along the finger, with possible loss of movement or numbness.\n\n**What to Do Next:**\n- If you heard a pop, see swelling, or notice bowstringing, consult a doctor—these can indicate a serious pulley or tendon injury.\n- Rest is crucial for both types; avoid climbing until pain subsides.\n\nWould you like guidance on self-assessment or advice on initial home care?", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:1": 1.0, "clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:2": 0.5, "clueweb22-en0012-60-11476:6": 0.3333333333333333, "clueweb22-en0014-41-15217:6": 0.25, "clueweb22-en0014-41-15217:5": 0.2}, "ptkb_provenance": ["I am climbing