{"metadata": {"team_id": "grilllab", "run_id": "grilllab-larf-finetuned-rankllm", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "3-1_5", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "Based on your description—pain in the front of the finger, especially when crimping, but no visible bowstringing—here’s what’s most relevant:\n\n**A2 Pulley Strain Is Still Likely**\n- Pain at the front of the finger during crimping is classic for an A2 pulley strain, even without visible bowstringing. Most A2 pulley injuries do not cause obvious bowstringing unless the rupture is complete; partial strains are much more common and present just as you describe.\n- Full crimp grip is the highest risk for this injury, which matches your climbing activity and symptom onset.\n\n**Other Causes Less Likely**\n- Collateral ligament injuries cause pain on the side of the finger, not the front.\n- Tendon injuries can cause pain, but are more often associated with wrist pain or issues after open injuries (cuts), which you haven’t mentioned.\n\n**What This Means for You**\n- Your symptoms fit a mild to moderate A2 pulley strain, the most common climbing finger injury.\n- Absence of bowstringing suggests it’s not a complete rupture, which is a good sign.\n\n**Next Steps**\n- Rest the finger and avoid crimping for now.\n- Consider taping the finger for support if you return to climbing.\n- Gradually reintroduce finger loading with gentle exercises once pain subsides.\n- If pain persists or worsens, or if you develop swelling, bruising, or loss of function, seek medical evaluation.\n\nWould you like specific guidance on taping techniques or rehab exercises for pulley injuries?", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0015-71-10781:16": 1.0, "clueweb22-en0015-71-10781:15": 0.5, "clueweb22-en0005-83-07260:9": 0.3333333333333333}, "ptkb_provenance": ["I am climbing regularly for the past 3 years.", "Many years ago I had a bad fall while bouldering.", "I climb outdoors."]}], "references": {"clueweb22-en0015-71-10781:16": 1.0, "clueweb22