{"metadata": {"team_id": "grilllab", "run_id": "grilllab-larf-finetuned-rankllm", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "3-1_6", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "Based on your description—pain in the front of the finger when crimping, no visible bowstringing—this is most consistent with a mild (Grade 1) A2 pulley strain, which is common in climbers and does not require immobilization if there’s no bowstringing or severe swelling.\n\n**Immediate Next Steps:**\n- **Stop climbing and avoid stressing the finger** (especially crimping) until pain subsides.\n- **Gentle range-of-motion exercises:** Bend and straighten the finger until you feel tightness (not pain), hold for 2–3 seconds, repeat 10 times, several times daily.\n- **Ice and NSAIDs** only if there is visible swelling.\n- **Light massage** of the finger and forearm flexor muscles can help blood flow and prevent scar tissue.\n- **No immobilization needed** unless pain worsens or you notice bowstringing.\n\n**When to Seek Medical Attention:**\n- If you develop visible bowstringing, significant swelling, or cannot bend the finger, see a climbing-knowledgeable physician for possible imaging (ultrasound/MRI).\n\n**Return to Climbing:**\n- Once pain-free with normal movement (usually 1–2 weeks), begin gentle re-strengthening (e.g., putty, stress ball).\n- Avoid crimping or painful grips for at least 6 weeks.\n- Full return to climbing is realistic in 6 weeks for mild injuries.\n\nWould you like specific taping techniques or advice on how to safely resume training?", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:7": 1.0, "clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:11": 0.5, "clueweb22-en0017-96-08323:4": 0.3333333333333333, "clueweb22-en0017-96-08323:3": 0.25, "clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:6": 0.2}, "ptkb_provenance": ["I am climbing regularly for the past 3 years.", "Many years ago I had a bad fall while bouldering.", "I climb outdoors."]}], "references": {"clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:7": 1.0, "clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:11": 0.5, "clueweb22-en