{"metadata": {"team_id": "grilllab", "run_id": "grilllab-larf-finetuned-rankllm", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "3-1_7", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "If you continue climbing without resting your injured finger, especially with pain in the A2 pulley area, you risk:\n\n- **Worsening the Injury:** Continued stress can turn a mild strain into a partial or complete pulley rupture, which may require months of recovery and could permanently affect finger strength and function.\n- **Longer Downtime:** Pushing through pain often leads to a more severe injury, resulting in significantly longer time off climbing than if you rest now.\n- **Chronic Issues:** Tendons and pulleys heal slowly due to limited blood flow. Overuse can lead to chronic pain, tendinosis, or persistent weakness, making future climbing less enjoyable and more injury-prone.\n\n**What’s recommended for climbers like you (3+ years experience):**\n- Rest the finger—avoid climbing or any activity that causes pain, especially crimping.\n- Expect at least 6 weeks for tendon or pulley injuries to heal, even minor ones.\n- Gentle range-of-motion exercises are safe if they don’t cause pain.\n- If pain persists or worsens, consult a climbing-savvy physician for assessment.\n\n**Why this matters for you:** As an experienced climber, you’re likely to push your limits. But tendons and pulleys need more recovery time than muscles. Ignoring pain now could mean much more time off the wall later.\n\nWould you like advice on specific rehab exercises or how to safely return to climbing after recovery?", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0017-96-08323:1": 1.0, "clueweb22-en0012-60-11476:7": 0.5, "clueweb22-en0021-66-06454:13": 0.3333333333333333, "clueweb22-en0012-60-11476:6": 0.25, "clueweb22-en0006-07-05326:1": 0.2}, "ptkb_provenance": ["I am climbing regularly for the past 3 years."]}], "references": {"clueweb22-en0017-96-08323:1": 1.0, "clueweb22-en0012-60-11476:7": 0.5, "clueweb22-en0021-66-06454:13": 0.333333333333