{"metadata": {"team_id": "grilllab", "run_id": "grilllab-larf-finetuned-rankllm", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "3-1_8", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "When returning to outdoor climbing after an A2 pulley injury, your main goal is to protect the healing tissue and prevent re-injury. Here’s a focused, actionable plan tailored to your situation:\n\n**1. Gradual Return & Route Selection**\n- Start with easy routes: Choose climbs with big holds and good footholds. Avoid overhangs, small crimps, and pockets, as these put high stress on the pulleys.\n- Keep sessions short: Limit your time on the wall to avoid fatigue, which increases injury risk.\n\n**2. Taping**\n- Use H-taping: This method provides the best support for injured pulleys, helping keep the tendon close to the bone and serving as a reminder to protect your finger.\n- Continue taping for several months, especially when climbing outdoors where holds are less predictable.\n\n**3. Progressive Loading**\n- Only increase difficulty if you experience no pain during or after easy climbing.\n- Avoid campus boards and high-intensity training until you’re fully pain-free.\n\n**4. Rehab & Recovery**\n- Maintain gentle range-of-motion and strengthening exercises (e.g., squeezing a stress ball, finger flexions) off the wall.\n- Light massage and alternating heat/cold can help with blood flow and recovery.\n\n**5. Listen to Your Body**\n- If pain returns, back off immediately. Complete tendon healing can take 100+ days.\n\n**Why this matters for outdoor climbers:** Outdoor holds are variable and sometimes sharp or tweaky, so err on the side of caution and prioritize routes that let you use open-hand grips.\n\nWould you like specific taping instructions or advice on finger rehab exercises to support your return?", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0017-96-08323:6": 1.0, "clueweb22-en0017-96-08323:5": 0.5, "clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:7": 0.3333333333333333, "clueweb22-en0015-71-10781:10": 0.25, "clueweb22-en0033-87-09727