{"metadata": {"team_id": "grilllab", "run_id": "grilllab-larf-finetuned-rankllm", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "3-1_10", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "H-taping is currently the most effective taping method for supporting injured finger pulleys—especially the A2 pulley—during climbing. Here’s what you need to know, tailored to your situation:\n\n**Why H-Taping?**\n- Research shows H-taping keeps the tendon closest to the bone under load, better than standard circular or X-taping.\n- It supports the injured pulley during flexion (bending), especially when crimping, and can increase crimping strength in injured fingers by about 13%.\n- It’s specifically recommended for climbers returning after pulley injuries.\n\n**How to Apply H-Tape (for A2 Pulley):**\n1. Cut a 10cm (about 4 inches) strip of rigid athletic tape, 1.5cm wide.\n2. From each end, split the tape lengthwise, leaving a 1cm “bridge” in the center.\n3. Place the bridge over the palmar side of the finger, right at the crease above the injured pulley.\n4. Flex your finger to about 30–60° at the joint.\n5. Wrap the upper and lower “legs” of the tape around the finger, forming two rings—one above and one below the joint.\n\n**Key Points:**\n- Use rigid tape (Leukoplast is preferred in studies).\n- Only tape the injured finger; taping healthy fingers is not recommended.\n- H-taping is most useful during the return-to-climbing phase, not as a preventive measure.\n\nWould you like a visual guide or tips on when to transition from taping back to normal climbing?", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:9": 1.0, "clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:8": 0.5, "clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:6": 0.3333333333333333, "clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:5": 0.25, "clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:10": 0.2}, "ptkb_provenance": ["I am climbing regularly for the past 3 years.", "I climb outdoors."]}], "references": {"clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:9": 1.0, "clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:8": 0.5, "clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:6": 0.3333333333333333, "clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:5": 0.25, "clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:10": 0.2, "clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:10": 0.16666666666666666, "clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:9": 0.14285714285714285, "clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:8": 0.125, "clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:7": 0.1111111111111111, "clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:12": 0.1, "clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:11": 0.09090909090909091, "clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:13": 0.08333333333333333, "clueweb22-en0007-93-01745:13": 0.07692307692307693, "clueweb22-en0003-54-05279:1": 0.07142857142857142}}
{"metadata": {"team_id": "grilllab", "run_id": "grilllab-larf-finetuned-rankllm", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "3-1_11", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "You asked whether you should tape other (uninjured) fingers when training.\n\n**Direct Answer:**  \nDo not tape healthy fingers as a preventative measure. Research shows that taping uninjured fingers does not provide meaningful support to healthy pulleys and may actually increase your risk of injury. Taping can prevent your pulleys from adapting and strengthening naturally, making them more vulnerable over time.\n\n**Key Points:**\n- **Taping is for injury support, not prevention:** Only tape the injured finger(s) to support healing and protect the pulley.  \n- **Risks of taping healthy fingers:** Prevents proper stress and adaptation of pulleys, potentially weakening them (Schweizer, 2000).\n- **Buddy taping (taping fingers together):** Not recommended for pulley injuries; it does not provide effective support and does not prevent bowstringing.\n\n**Best Practice for Your Situation:**  \n- Continue using H-taping or immobilization taping only on your injured finger while you recover and as you ease back into climbing.\n- Retape between routes if needed, as tape can loosen quickly.\n- Let your healthy fingers remain untaped so they can adapt and strengthen naturally.\n\nWould you like a quick visual guide or video link for H-taping, or tips on how to monitor your recovery as you return to climbing?", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:6": 1.0, "clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:1": 0.5, "clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:10": 0.3333333333333333, "clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:11": 0.25, "clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:14": 0.2}, "ptkb_provenance": ["I am climbing regularly for the past 3 years.", "Many years ago I had a bad fall while bouldering.", "I climb outdoors."]}], "references": {"clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:6": 1.0, "clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:1": 0.5, "clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:10": 0.3333333333333333, "clueweb22-en00