{"metadata": {"team_id": "grilllab", "run_id": "grilllab-larf-finetuned-rankllm", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "4-1_3", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "Here’s how to confidently prepare for your first 10K, tailored to your current fitness and goals:\n\n**1. Start Gradually, Focus on Consistency**\n- Aim for 3 runs per week, starting with run/walk intervals if needed. Even 15 minutes per session is a good start.\n- Most runs should be at a comfortable, “chatting” pace to build endurance and reduce injury risk.\n- Increase your total weekly distance by no more than 10–15% to avoid overtraining.\n\n**2. Build Up Long Run Distance**\n- Each week, make one run a little longer, working up to at least 7.5 km (about 75% of race distance) before race day.\n- Don’t worry about speed yet—just getting used to the distance is key.\n\n**3. Allow for Rest and Recovery**\n- Schedule rest days between runs to let your muscles recover. This is crucial for beginners and helps prevent burnout.\n\n**4. Cross-Train for Balance**\n- Add a low-impact cardio session (like swimming or cycling) once a week. This boosts fitness and aids recovery without stressing your legs.\n\n**5. Use a Simple Training Plan**\n- An 8-week plan is ideal for beginners. This gives your body time to adapt and builds confidence.\n\n**Why this works for you:**  \nYou’re 22, new to running, and want to avoid injury or discouragement. This approach is proven to help beginners succeed and enjoy the process.\n\nWould you like a sample 8-week beginner 10K plan, or tips on nutrition and gear for your runs?", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0004-91-11430:1": 1.0, "clueweb22-en0033-03-08847:2": 0.5, "clueweb22-en0036-22-19114:1": 0.3333333333333333, "clueweb22-en0034-83-04863:5": 0.25, "clueweb22-en0036-22-19114:2": 0.2}, "ptkb_provenance": ["I am not very confident in my fitness level.", "I am 22 years old.", "I live in Spain."]}], "references": {"clueweb22-en0004-91-11430:1": 1.0, "clueweb22-en0033-03-08847:2": 0.5