{"metadata": {"team_id": "grilllab", "run_id": "grilllab-larf-finetuned-rankllm", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "4-1_5", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "Here’s a typical, effective 10K training week for a runner preparing for a spring race in New York:\n\n**Weekly Structure (5 Days Running, 2 Rest/Cross-Train):**\n\n- **Monday: Rest or Cross-Train**\n  - Essential for muscle recovery and injury prevention.\n\n- **Tuesday: Easy Run**\n  - 5–7 km at a comfortable pace (should be able to hold a conversation).\n\n- **Wednesday: Speedwork/Intervals**\n  - Example: 5 x 800m at 10K pace with 2–3 min easy jog recovery.\n  - Builds speed and race-specific fitness.\n\n- **Thursday: Rest or Easy Run**\n  - 5–6 km, relaxed pace, or take a full rest day if needed.\n\n- **Friday: Tempo or Pace Run**\n  - 6–8 km including 3–5 km at your target 10K pace.\n  - Teaches your body to sustain race effort.\n\n- **Saturday: Long Run**\n  - 8–12 km at a steady, easy pace (1–2 min/km slower than race pace).\n  - Builds endurance.\n\n- **Sunday: Recovery Run**\n  - 4–6 km, very easy pace, just to keep the legs moving.\n\n**Key Points for Your Context:**\n- April in New York is cool and ideal for running—dress in light layers.\n- Hydration is still important, but fueling mid-run is usually unnecessary for 10K.\n- Adjust rest days based on how your body feels.\n\nWould you like a sample 8- or 12-week progression plan, or tips on how to adjust this template for your current fitness level?", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0032-17-06111:4": 1.0, "clueweb22-en0002-42-01192:0": 0.5, "clueweb22-en0014-61-01516:3": 0.3333333333333333, "clueweb22-en0011-25-10165:1": 0.25, "clueweb22-en0044-52-00733:0": 0.2}, "ptkb_provenance": ["I am not very confident in my fitness level.", "I am 22 years old."]}], "references": {"clueweb22-en0032-17-06111:4": 1.0, "clueweb22-en0002-42-01192:0": 0.5, "clueweb22-en0014-61-01516:3": 0.3333333333333333, "clueweb22-en0011-25-10165:1": 0.25, "clueweb22-en0044-52-007