{"metadata": {"team_id": "grilllab", "run_id": "grilllab-larf-finetuned-rankllm", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "4-1_6", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "With four months to prepare for your 10km run in New York next April, you have ample time to build fitness, reduce injury risk, and peak on race day. Here’s how to structure your training for maximum benefit:\n\n**1. Progressive Training Structure**\n- **12-16 Weeks:** Most effective 10k plans span 8-12 weeks, but with 16 weeks, you can build gradually and safely.\n- **Every 4th Week = Recovery:** Halve your training load every fourth week. This “active recovery” prevents burnout and injuries ([1], [3]).\n- **Weekly Mileage:** Start low and increase by no more than 10% per week ([3]). Example: If you run 15 miles one week, next week aim for ~16.5 miles.\n\n**2. Weekly Workout Template**\n- **3-4 Runs/Week:** Mix easy runs, intervals (e.g., 5x1600m at 10k pace), tempo runs (e.g., 2x10min), and a weekly long run (45-60 min).\n- **2 Rest Days:** Essential for recovery, especially for beginners/intermediates ([2], [3]).\n- **Strength Training:** Add 1-2 sessions/week (40-45 min each) for injury prevention and better running economy ([1], [16]).\n\n**3. Personalization for Your Timeline**\n- **First 4 Weeks:** Focus on easy runs and building consistency.\n- **Middle 8 Weeks:** Introduce intervals, tempo, and longer runs.\n- **Final 4 Weeks:** Sharpen with race-pace work, then taper in the last 7-10 days.\n\n**4. Weather & Hydration**\n- April in NYC is cool (avg. 16°C), ideal for running. Hydrate, but fueling mid-run is not critical for 10k.\n\nWould you like a detailed 16-week plan, or tips on pacing and race-day strategy?", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0004-91-11430:5": 1.0, "clueweb22-en0032-17-06111:4": 0.5, "clueweb22-en0004-91-11430:16": 0.3333333333333333, "clueweb22-en0005-33-13481:0": 0.25, "clueweb22-en0033-30-18644:7": 0.2}, "ptkb_provenance": ["I am not very confident in my fitness level.", "I am 22 y