{"metadata": {"team_id": "grilllab", "run_id": "grilllab-larf-finetuned-rankllm", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "4-1_7", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "To estimate your lactate threshold for 10km training—without lab testing—use a simple field test:\n\n**DIY Lactate Threshold Test (30-Minute Time Trial):**\n- Warm up thoroughly.\n- Run for 30 minutes at the hardest, sustainable pace you can maintain evenly (not a sprint, but a hard effort).\n- Use a heart rate monitor if possible. Ignore your heart rate for the first 10 minutes.\n- For the final 20 minutes, record your average heart rate. This is your estimated lactate threshold heart rate (LT HR).\n- If you don’t have a monitor, take your pulse at 10 and 30 minutes, average the two.\n\n**Effort-Based Estimate:**\n- Your threshold pace should feel like 8–8.5 out of 10 on effort—hard, but sustainable for up to an hour.\n- This is often called “comfortably hard”—you can’t speak more than a word or two at a time.\n\n**Why This Matters for You:**\n- Knowing your threshold helps you target tempo runs and intervals at the right intensity, maximizing your 10km performance.\n- With four months to train, you can repeat this test every 4–6 weeks to track progress and adjust your training zones.\n\nWould you like guidance on how to use your threshold heart rate or pace to structure your workouts?", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0028-06-06729:11": 1.0, "clueweb22-en0041-79-02034:2": 0.5, "clueweb22-en0028-06-06729:10": 0.3333333333333333, "clueweb22-en0026-34-14075:0": 0.25, "clueweb22-en0046-47-18551:17": 0.2}, "ptkb_provenance": ["I am not very confident in my fitness level."]}], "references": {"clueweb22-en0028-06-06729:11": 1.0, "clueweb22-en0041-79-02034:2": 0.5, "clueweb22-en0028-06-06729:10": 0.3333333333333333, "clueweb22-en0026-34-14075:0": 0.25, "clueweb22-en0046-47-18551:17": 0.2, "clueweb22-en0003-17-08043:5": 0.16666666666666666, "clueweb22-en0033-86-01487:3": 0.14285714285714285, "clueweb22-en0019-25-12288