{"metadata": {"team_id": "grilllab", "run_id": "grilllab-larf-finetuned-rankllm", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "4-1_12", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "Switching from a 10K to a 5K will change your training in several key ways, mainly by reducing overall mileage and shifting the focus toward speed and recovery.\n\n**Key Differences in 5K vs. 10K Training:**\n\n- **Duration & Intensity:** 5K plans are shorter (often 4–8 weeks) and emphasize speed and interval work. You’ll do more sessions at or above your goal pace, with less emphasis on long, slow runs.\n- **Weekly Structure:** Typical 5K training involves 3 runs per week:\n  - 1 interval/speed session (e.g., short repeats at 5K pace)\n  - 1 tempo or steady run (comfortably hard, but not all-out)\n  - 1 longer run (but usually only 40–60 minutes)\n  - Rest or cross-training on other days (walking, cycling, or strength work)\n- **Recovery:** More frequent rest or easy days, since the intensity is higher.\n- **Cross-Training:** Still recommended for overall fitness and injury prevention.\n\n**Sample 5K Week:**\n- Tuesday: 30 min run (intervals or tempo)\n- Thursday: 30 min easy run\n- Saturday: 40–60 min long run\n- Other days: rest, walk, or cross-train\n\n**Personalization for You:**\n- Since you have 4 months, you can build a strong base with easy runs and cross-training, then shift to focused 5K training 6–8 weeks before the race.\n- If you’re new to speed work, introduce it gradually to avoid injury.\n\nWould you like a detailed 5K training plan tailored to your current fitness and schedule?", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0019-16-15604:1": 1.0, "clueweb22-en0022-95-09628:5": 0.5, "clueweb22-en0005-48-11149:1": 0.3333333333333333, "clueweb22-en0012-56-09278:10": 0.25, "clueweb22-en0022-10-14457:8": 0.2}, "ptkb_provenance": ["I am not very confident in my fitness level."]}], "references": {"clueweb22-en0019-16-15604:1": 1.0, "clueweb22-en0022-95-09628:5": 0.5, "clueweb22-en0005-48-11149:1": 0.333333333333