{"metadata": {"team_id": "grilllab", "run_id": "grilllab-larf-finetuned-rankllm", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "4-2_11", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "For training in New York parks, here are the best options tailored to your needs—focusing on size, running amenities, and variety:\n\n**Central Park (Manhattan)**\n- **Size:** ~845 acres (3.4 sq km)\n- **Why train here:** Iconic, vast, and centrally located. Offers multiple running loops (including a 6-mile perimeter), the Reservoir track, and varied terrain. Great for both short and long runs, with plenty of scenic spots and amenities.\n\n**Van Cortlandt Park (Bronx)**\n- **Size:** One of NYC’s largest parks\n- **Running options:** 1.25-mile rubber track and a renowned 3-mile cross-country trail—ideal for serious training or trail running.\n\n**Randall’s Island Park (Manhattan/East River)**\n- **Icahn Stadium:** 400-meter professional Mondo track, used for Olympic-level events. Also features synthetic soccer fields and river views. Less crowded, excellent for focused workouts.\n\n**Macombs Dam Park (Bronx)**\n- **Track:** State-of-the-art 400-meter track (Olympic-quality surface), plus turf fields and fitness areas. Located near Yankee Stadium.\n\n**Other Notable Parks with Tracks**\n- **Astoria Park (Queens):** 0.25-mile all-weather track, river views.\n- **McCarren Park (Brooklyn):** 0.25-mile rubber track, lively neighborhood vibe.\n\n**Recommendation:**  \nIf you want variety and iconic scenery, Central Park is unbeatable. For a dedicated track session, Icahn Stadium or Macombs Dam Park offer top facilities. For trail running, Van Cortlandt Park is best.\n\nWould you like details on park locations, best times to train, or how to access these spots by subway?", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0032-26-02648:7": 1.0, "clueweb22-en0046-58-10177:0": 0.5, "clueweb22-en0033-30-10729:0": 0.3333333333333333, "clueweb22-en0039-55-13283:0": 0.25, "clueweb22-en0042-85-01435:3": 0.2}, "ptkb_provenance": ["I am not ver