{"metadata": {"team_id": "grilllab", "run_id": "grilllab-larf-finetuned-rankllm", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "5-2_5", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "Congratulations again! Here’s what you need to know about diet during pregnancy, tailored to your preferences:\n\n**General Principles**\n- You do NOT need to “eat for two.” Only in the last trimester do you need about 200 extra calories per day.\n- Focus on a balanced diet: plenty of vegetables and fruit, whole grains, lean proteins, and dairy. Limit foods high in fat and sugar.\n\n**Low-Carb Diets**\n- Strict low-carb diets (like Atkins or South Beach) are NOT recommended during pregnancy. Your body and baby need complex carbohydrates for energy and nutrients.\n- Instead, choose healthy carbs: whole grains (brown rice, whole-wheat bread/pasta), fruits, and vegetables. These provide fiber and help prevent constipation.\n\n**Your Usual Foods**\n- **Jam for breakfast:** Fine in moderation, but watch added sugars. Pair with wholegrain toast for better nutrition.\n- **White rice for lunch:** Consider swapping some meals to brown rice or other whole grains for more fiber and nutrients.\n- **Cake as a snack:** Enjoy occasionally, but try to limit frequency and portion size due to high sugar and fat.\n- **Fresh orange juice:** Okay sometimes, but whole fruit is better (more fiber, less sugar spike).\n\n**Key Nutrients**\n- Ensure you get enough folic acid, iron, calcium, and iodine—these are especially important in pregnancy.\n\n**Food Safety**\n- Wash all fruits and vegetables thoroughly.\n- Avoid certain foods (e.g., unpasteurized dairy, some deli meats, high-mercury fish).\n\nWould you like a sample meal plan or tips for healthier baking and snack swaps during pregnancy?", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0000-09-19416:0": 1.0, "clueweb22-en0012-37-11383:13": 0.5, "clueweb22-en0023-54-04375:3": 0.3333333333333333, "clueweb22-en0027-89-15500:1": 0.25, "clueweb22-en0008-96-17009:15": 0.2}, "ptkb_provenance": ["I thin