{"metadata": {"team_id": "grilllab", "run_id": "grilllab-larf-finetuned-rankllm", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "5-2_8", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "You are very unlikely to \"run out\" of milk for your baby. True, many new mothers worry about low milk supply, but actual insufficient production is rare if you breastfeed on demand and your baby is latching well.\n\n**Key Points:**\n\n- **Normal Supply:** Most mothers produce enough milk for their baby's needs. Perceived low supply is often due to normal baby behaviors (like frequent feeding or fussiness), not actual lack of milk.\n- **What Causes Low Supply?**\n  - Infrequent feeding or scheduled feeds (not feeding on demand)\n  - Poor latch or attachment\n  - Use of formula or skipping breastfeeds without expressing milk\n  - Certain medications (especially those with estrogen), smoking, or some health conditions (e.g., diabetes, thyroid issues)\n  - Rapid weight loss or sudden drop in calorie intake\n- **How to Know Baby Gets Enough:**\n  - Baby has plenty of wet nappies/diapers daily\n  - Steady weight gain\n  - Baby seems satisfied after feeds\n\n**What You Can Do:**\n- Feed your baby whenever they show signs of hunger (on demand, not by the clock)\n- Ensure a good latch\n- Avoid unnecessary formula supplementation unless medically advised\n- Stay hydrated and eat a balanced diet\n\nIf you ever have concerns, tracking wet diapers and weight gain is the best reassurance. If you notice a real drop in supply or your baby isn’t gaining weight, consult a lactation consultant or your healthcare provider.\n\nWould you like tips on boosting supply or signs of a good latch?", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0019-48-05136:2": 1.0, "clueweb22-en0021-55-01275:1": 0.5, "clueweb22-en0035-54-11093:6": 0.3333333333333333, "clueweb22-en0011-28-08023:3": 0.25, "clueweb22-en0021-55-01275:0": 0.2}, "ptkb_provenance": ["I think I don't get enough calcium, and I am worried about it.", "I eat jam for breakfast.", "I like eatin