{"metadata": {"team_id": "grilllab", "run_id": "grilllab-larf-finetuned-rankllm", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "5-2_9", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "Congratulations again! Here’s what you need to know as you prepare for your new arrival and breastfeeding journey:\n\n**Breastfeeding Essentials**\n- **Start Early:** Begin breastfeeding within the first hour after birth. This helps your baby get colostrum (the first milk, rich in nutrients and antibodies) and signals your body to start producing more milk.\n- **Exclusive Breastfeeding:** For the first 6 months, breast milk alone is recommended—no water, formula, or other foods.\n- **Milk Supply:** Your body makes milk based on demand. The more your baby nurses, the more milk you’ll produce. If you’re worried about supply, offer extra feeds—especially during growth spurts (often around 2 weeks, 6 weeks, and 3 months).\n\n**Getting Comfortable**\n- **Latch and Position:** A good latch is key to comfort and milk transfer. If you’re unsure, ask for help from a lactation consultant or your midwife.\n- **Skin-to-Skin Contact:** Holding your baby close, especially skin-to-skin, helps with bonding and milk production.\n- **Watch Baby, Not the Clock:** Feed whenever your baby shows hunger cues (rooting, sucking, fussiness), not on a strict schedule.\n\n**Self-Care**\n- **Rest and Nourishment:** Prioritize your own sleep and nutrition. Breastfeeding is demanding—eat a balanced diet and drink plenty of fluids.\n- **Support:** Breastfeeding can be challenging at first. Don’t hesitate to reach out for help—midwives, lactation consultants, and helplines are there for you.\n\nWould you like tips on preparing for the first days at home, or advice on common breastfeeding challenges?", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0002-06-10976:0": 1.0, "clueweb22-en0002-16-03599:2": 0.5, "clueweb22-en0021-49-11932:18": 0.3333333333333333, "clueweb22-en0044-58-01018:9": 0.25, "clueweb22-en0002-16-03599:1": 0.2}, "ptkb_provenance": ["I think 