{"metadata": {"team_id": "grilllab", "run_id": "grilllab-larf-finetuned-rankllm", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "5-2_10", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "Congratulations again on your pregnancy and your excitement about being a caring mum! Here’s what you need to know about newborn crying, tailored to your concern about your baby’s comfort and your hope to minimize distress:\n\n**Crying Is Normal and Healthy**\n- Newborns typically cry 2–3 hours per day in the first few months. This is their main way to communicate needs like hunger, tiredness, or needing a diaper change.\n- Some babies may have periods of “PURPLE crying” (Peak, Unexpected, Resists soothing, Pain-like face, Long-lasting, Evening), which can last up to five hours a day. This is a normal developmental stage, not a sign of something wrong.\n\n**You Can Help Soothe Your Baby**\n- Responding quickly and lovingly to your baby’s cries (ideally within 90 seconds) can actually reduce overall crying.\n- Feeding on demand, especially if breastfeeding, and keeping your baby close (in your arms or a carrier) for several hours a day can help your baby feel secure and cry less.\n- Babies also cry for comfort and cuddles, not just for food or diaper changes.\n\n**You’re Not Alone**\n- Even with the best care, all babies will cry sometimes. It’s not a reflection on your parenting.\n- If you ever feel overwhelmed, it’s okay to take a short break while ensuring your baby is safe.\n\nWould you like tips on reading your baby’s cues or soothing techniques for those tougher moments?", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0043-40-01751:2": 1.0, "clueweb22-en0022-43-13342:10": 0.5, "clueweb22-en0000-45-00661:0": 0.3333333333333333, "clueweb22-en0016-87-16257:3": 0.25, "clueweb22-en0006-80-13155:3": 0.2}, "ptkb_provenance": ["I am a woman."]}], "references": {"clueweb22-en0043-40-01751:2": 1.0, "clueweb22-en0022-43-13342:10": 0.5, "clueweb22-en0000-45-00661:0": 0.3333333333333333, "clueweb22-en0016-87-16257:3": 0.25, "cluewe