{"metadata": {"team_id": "grilllab", "run_id": "grilllab-larf-finetuned-rankllm", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "7-1_9", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "For fatty liver, the healthiest homemade bread options are those high in fiber, low in refined carbs, and free from added sugars or preservatives. Here’s what best fits your needs:\n\n**Top Recommendation: Rye & Whole Wheat Bread (Homemade)**\n- **Why:** Rye bread is repeatedly cited as the best option for fatty liver due to its high fiber and lower carb content, which support digestion and stable blood sugar. Mixing rye with whole wheat flour improves taste and texture while keeping it healthy.\n- **How to Make:** Combine rye flour (150g) with whole wheat flour (200g), add dry yeast, salt, and water. Bake at home to avoid store-bought additives.\n- **Taste Tip:** Pure rye can be dense and bitter; blending with whole wheat makes it more palatable.\n\n**Other Healthy Options:**\n- **Sprouted Grain Bread:** Made from sprouted whole grains, offers more nutrients and a lower glycemic impact. Harder to bake at home, but excellent if you can source or make it.\n- **Keto Bread:** Low-carb, but most store-bought versions contain preservatives. Only recommended if you make it yourself with clean ingredients.\n\n**What to Avoid:**\n- White bread and most commercial breads, as they spike blood sugar and often contain unhealthy additives.\n\n**Portion Guidance:** Even with healthy bread, limit intake to 2–3 slices per day to support liver recovery.\n\nWould you like a simple step-by-step recipe for rye & whole wheat bread, or tips on sourcing quality ingredients?", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0046-25-14652:5": 1.0, "clueweb22-en0046-25-14652:7": 0.5, "clueweb22-en0046-25-14652:11": 0.3333333333333333, "clueweb22-en0046-25-14652:2": 0.25, "clueweb22-en0046-25-14652:10": 0.2}, "ptkb_provenance": ["I have some basic baking experience.", "I enjoy baking."]}], "references": {"clueweb22-en0046-25-14652:5": 1.0, "clueweb22-en