{"metadata": {"team_id": "grilllab", "run_id": "grilllab-larf-finetuned-rankllm", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "8-1_1", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "Managing ADHD in an office setting is absolutely possible with targeted strategies. Here’s a distilled, actionable guide tailored to your work environment in Germany:\n\n**Minimize Distractions**\n- Use noise-cancelling headphones or play white noise/music to block out office sounds.\n- If possible, request a quieter workspace or use unused meeting rooms for focused tasks.\n- Route calls to voicemail and check them at set times to avoid interruptions.\n\n**Stay Organized**\n- Keep your workspace clutter-free; assign specific spots for essential items.\n- Use digital or physical to-do lists and checklists to track tasks and deadlines.\n- Take notes during meetings—this helps with memory and channels restlessness productively.\n\n**Time & Task Management**\n- Break large projects into smaller, manageable steps.\n- Set clear, short deadlines—consider asking a colleague or supervisor to help with accountability.\n- Use reminders (sticky notes, phone alerts) for important tasks and deadlines.\n\n**Combat Boredom & Restlessness**\n- Alternate between different types of tasks to maintain engagement.\n- Take short, regular breaks to move around—use lunch breaks for a walk or light exercise.\n- If meetings are long, take notes or doodle discreetly to stay attentive.\n\n**Social & Communication Skills**\n- Ask for feedback from colleagues to improve interactions.\n- Practice active listening and be mindful of interrupting others.\n\nThese strategies are practical for a German office environment and can be implemented without major workplace changes. Would you like tips on digital tools or apps that support these strategies, or advice on discussing accommodations with your employer?", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0019-85-03855:10": 1.0, "clueweb22-en0025-75-16053:1": 0.5, "clueweb22-en0017-60-15663:0": 0.33333333333333