{"metadata": {"team_id": "grilllab", "run_id": "grilllab-larf-finetuned-rankllm", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "8-1_2", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "Feeling overwhelmed by too many tasks is a common ADHD challenge, especially in an office setting. Here’s how you can manage this effectively:\n\n**1. Prioritize Ruthlessly**\n- Break your workload into smaller, manageable tasks. Focus on just 1–3 top priorities per day.\n- Use checklists or visual task boards (digital or paper) to keep your priorities visible and actionable.\n\n**2. Set Clear Boundaries**\n- Avoid overcommitting. Practice saying no or delegating tasks when possible to prevent overload.\n- Block out time on your calendar for focused work and protect it from meetings or interruptions.\n\n**3. Use Timers and Reminders**\n- Set timers for work sprints (e.g., 25 minutes on, 5 minutes off) to maintain momentum and prevent burnout.\n- Schedule reminders for deadlines and meetings, giving yourself extra buffer time.\n\n**4. Take Strategic Breaks**\n- Short, regular breaks help reset your focus and reduce overwhelm. Try walking, stretching, or a brief meditation.\n\n**5. Reward Progress**\n- Celebrate small wins—checking off a task, completing a project—with a treat or short enjoyable activity.\n\n**6. Organize Your Space**\n- Keep your workspace clutter-free to minimize visual overwhelm and make it easier to find what you need.\n\nThese strategies are tailored for a quiet office and directly address feeling overwhelmed by task volume. Would you like specific tools or templates for task lists and prioritization?", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0001-03-06965:6": 1.0, "clueweb22-en0001-03-06965:5": 0.5, "clueweb22-en0009-34-06191:4": 0.3333333333333333, "clueweb22-en0024-69-14725:7": 0.25, "clueweb22-en0036-02-14625:6": 0.2}, "ptkb_provenance": ["I love it when it is quiet.", "I live in Germany.", "I work in an office."]}], "references": {"clueweb22-en0001-03-06965:6": 1.0, "clueweb22-en0001-03-06965:5