{"metadata": {"team_id": "grilllab", "run_id": "grilllab-larf-finetuned-rankllm", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "8-1_4", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "You’re experiencing overwhelm and task-switching—common ADHD challenges, especially when faced with multiple priorities. Here’s how to address this, tailored to your situation:\n\n**1. Monotasking Over Multitasking**\n- Focus on one task at a time. Multitasking is especially draining for ADHD brains and leads to unfinished work.\n- Keep your daily to-do list short (ideally 3–4 items). Hide the rest until you finish the current task.\n\n**2. Structured Task Switching**\n- If you find it impossible to stick to one task, limit yourself to two. Allow yourself to switch between them when your attention wanes, but don’t add more.\n- Use scheduled blocks (e.g., Pomodoro Technique: 25 minutes on, 5 minutes off) to create clear boundaries for each task.\n\n**3. Externalize and Prioritize**\n- Write down all tasks, then pick the most urgent or important. Physically move other tasks out of sight—this reduces overwhelm.\n- If you’re a list person, only keep the current task visible.\n\n**4. Learn Your Personal Rhythm**\n- Notice when your focus is best and plan demanding tasks for those times.\n- Accept that your process may be non-linear—what matters is finishing, not the order.\n\n**5. Consider ADHD Coaching**\n- A coach can help you develop personalized strategies, stay accountable, and reduce anxiety around unfinished tasks.\n\nWould you like a sample daily workflow or tools (apps, planners) that support monotasking and prioritization?", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0021-33-10264:5": 1.0, "clueweb22-en0020-61-13551:10": 0.5, "clueweb22-en0036-02-14625:4": 0.3333333333333333, "clueweb22-en0036-02-14625:5": 0.25, "clueweb22-en0034-25-12642:4": 0.2}, "ptkb_provenance": ["I live in Germany.", "I work in an office."]}], "references": {"clueweb22-en0021-33-10264:5": 1.0, "clueweb22-en0020-61-13551:10": 0.5, "clueweb22-en0