{"metadata": {"team_id": "grilllab", "run_id": "grilllab-larf-finetuned-rankllm", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "8-1_5", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "To address your challenge—difficulty focusing on one task and jumping between tasks—here’s a targeted, ADHD-friendly strategy that fits your office, sedentary work environment:\n\n**Timed Single-Task Focus (Pomodoro/Chunking Method)**\n- **How it works:** Pick just one task to focus on. Set a timer (start with 20–30 minutes). Work only on that task until the timer rings. Then take a short break (5 minutes). If your attention wanes, allow yourself to switch to a second pre-chosen task for the next timed session.\n- **Why it helps:** The timer creates a clear boundary, making it easier for your brain to “commit” to one task. Short bursts reduce overwhelm and help you finish more.\n- **Tools:** Use a simple kitchen timer, your phone, or visual timer apps (like Forest or Time Timer) that show time passing—this visual cue is especially helpful for ADHD brains.\n- **Bonus tip:** If you struggle to get started, tell yourself you only have to work until the timer rings—no pressure to finish the whole task.\n\n**Why this fits you:**\n- Minimal setup—just a timer or app, no extra gadgets.\n- Works well in a quiet office and doesn’t require physical activity.\n- Reduces overwhelm by narrowing your focus to one thing at a time.\n\nWould you like a step-by-step guide for setting this up, or suggestions for timer apps that work well in an office setting?", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0026-06-01592:6": 1.0, "clueweb22-en0035-93-06191:4": 0.5, "clueweb22-en0007-41-01613:0": 0.3333333333333333, "clueweb22-en0017-11-11118:12": 0.25, "clueweb22-en0030-53-01118:10": 0.2}, "ptkb_provenance": ["I have a sedentary lifestyle.", "I live in Germany.", "I work in an office."]}], "references": {"clueweb22-en0026-06-01592:6": 1.0, "clueweb22-en0035-93-06191:4": 0.5, "clueweb22-en0007-41-01613:0": 0.3333333333333333, "clueweb22-en0017-1