{"metadata": {"team_id": "grilllab", "run_id": "grilllab-larf-finetuned-rankllm", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "8-1_6", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "You’re looking for ADHD-friendly work strategies that don’t interrupt your hyperfocus or feel too rigid—unlike the Pomodoro Technique. Here’s what works best for your needs:\n\n**1. Flowtime Technique: Flexible Focus**\n- Instead of fixed intervals, you start working and only note the time when you begin and when your focus naturally wanes.\n- Take a break when you feel your attention slipping, not when a timer tells you.\n- This lets you ride out periods of hyperfocus without interruption, but still encourages regular breaks to prevent burnout.\n- Especially useful if you dislike alarms or want to respect your natural attention rhythms.\n\n**2. Task Switching with Structure**\n- Pick two tasks to alternate between. When your focus fades on one, switch to the other.\n- This leverages your brain’s need for novelty without overwhelming you with too many options.\n- Scheduling just one or two tasks per block (rather than a long to-do list) can make your day feel more manageable.\n\n**3. Visual or Gentle Transition Cues**\n- If you do use timers, set a gentle pre-warning (e.g., 10 minutes before you want to stop) to help you transition out of hyperfocus.\n- Physical movement—standing up, changing seats—can also help break the “trance” without feeling abrupt.\n\n**Why these work for you:**  \nThey respect your natural focus patterns and avoid the rigidity that disrupts your flow.\n\nWould you like a quick guide on setting up the Flowtime Technique, or tips for structuring your task list?", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0038-55-03763:1": 1.0, "clueweb22-en0036-02-14625:4": 0.5, "clueweb22-en0036-02-14625:3": 0.3333333333333333, "clueweb22-en0039-55-00787:11": 0.25, "clueweb22-en0006-66-05593:7": 0.2}, "ptkb_provenance": ["I have a sedentary lifestyle.", "I live in Germany.", "I work in an office."]}], "references":