{"metadata": {"team_id": "grilllab", "run_id": "grilllab-larf-finetuned-rankllm", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "8-1_7", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "To protect your hyperfocus in the office—especially from emails and meetings—while still staying responsive, try these ADHD-friendly strategies:\n\n**1. Control Email Distractions**\n- **Batch Checking:** Set specific times to check email (e.g., 9am, 1pm, 4pm) instead of keeping your inbox open all day. This prevents constant interruptions and lets you dive deep into tasks.\n- **Notifications Off:** Silence email and messaging alerts during focus periods. If your job requires responsiveness, communicate your “focus windows” to colleagues so they know when you’ll reply.\n\n**2. Manage Meetings and Scheduled Interruptions**\n- **Time Blocking:** Reserve certain hours or days for meetings (e.g., only Mondays and Fridays), leaving other blocks as interruption-free zones for deep work.\n- **Calendar Buffers:** Schedule short buffer periods before and after meetings to help you transition smoothly and avoid abrupt breaks in focus.\n\n**3. Optimize Your Environment**\n- **Single-Task Mode:** Close unnecessary browser tabs and apps. Use website blockers (like RescueTime) during focus sessions to prevent accidental distractions.\n- **Atmosphere:** Adjust your workspace to your preference—white noise, music, or silence—to help you stay in the zone.\n\n**4. Handling Unavoidable Interruptions**\n- **Quick Notes:** If interrupted, jot down exactly where you left off. This makes it easier to re-enter your flow after the distraction.\n\nThese approaches are tailored for an office setting and respect your need for both deep focus and flexibility. Would you like tips on communicating your focus needs to your team, or tools to help automate some of these strategies?", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0014-37-11521:3": 1.0, "clueweb22-en0030-54-15087:19": 0.5, "clueweb22-en0006-62-03234:3": 0.3333333333333333, "clueweb22-en0022-64