{"metadata": {"team_id": "grilllab", "run_id": "grilllab-larf-finetuned-rankllm", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "8-1_8", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "To help you manage meetings with ADHD in your office environment, here are the most effective, actionable strategies:\n\n**1. Prepare and Structure Meetings**\n- **Keep notes:** Bring a notepad to jot down key points or distracting thoughts. This helps you stay engaged and provides an outlet for restlessness (clueweb22-en0019-85-03855).\n- **Set an agenda:** If possible, ask for or create a clear agenda before the meeting. This gives your attention a roadmap and reduces anxiety about what’s coming next.\n\n**2. Manage Attention During Meetings**\n- **Allow yourself to stim:** Subtle, repetitive actions (like doodling or fidgeting) can actually help you stay focused. If you need to, explain to colleagues that this helps you engage, not distracts you (clueweb22-en0008-36-12963).\n- **Batch meetings:** If you have control over your schedule, try to cluster meetings on specific days. This leaves other days free for deep, uninterrupted work (clueweb22-en0006-62-03234).\n\n**3. Minimize Meeting Disruption**\n- **Plan focused work around meetings:** Schedule your most demanding tasks for times when you don’t have meetings, so interruptions don’t break your flow (clueweb22-en0006-62-03234).\n- **Avoid over-scheduling:** Don’t stack too many meetings in a row. Give yourself breaks to reset and avoid overwhelm (clueweb22-en0019-85-03855).\n\n**4. After the Meeting**\n- **Review your notes:** Quickly summarize action items right after the meeting to reinforce memory and clarify next steps.\n\nWould you like tips on communicating your needs to colleagues or more on managing transitions between meetings and focused work?", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0025-75-16053:1": 1.0, "clueweb22-en0006-62-03234:3": 0.5, "clueweb22-en0008-36-12963:1": 0.3333333333333333, "clueweb22-en0019-85-03855:11": 0.25, "clueweb22-en0019-85-038