{"metadata": {"team_id": "grilllab", "run_id": "grilllab-larf-finetuned-rankllm", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "8-1_9", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "Meetings as Distractions: ADHD-Friendly Strategies\n\nYour core challenge is that scheduled meetings interrupt your hyperfocus and workflow, not that you struggle to participate in them. Here’s how to minimize their disruptive impact:\n\n**1. Batch and Compartmentalize Meetings**\n- If possible, group meetings into specific days or time blocks (e.g., all meetings on Mondays and Fridays). This leaves other days mostly free for deep, uninterrupted work. This approach is especially effective for ADHD brains, as it reduces the number of context switches and helps you anticipate when focus will be interrupted.\n\n**2. Use Transition Cues**\n- Set visible or audible reminders (alarms, phone notifications) 10–15 minutes before a meeting. This helps you “land the plane” on your current task, reducing the jarring effect of sudden transitions.\n- Place the alarm across the room so you must physically get up—this movement helps break hyperfocus gently and prepares you for the meeting.\n\n**3. Communicate Your Focus Needs**\n- Let colleagues know when you’re in a deep work block and when you’ll be available. This can train your team to avoid unscheduled interruptions and respect your focus time.\n\n**4. Prepare for Re-Entry**\n- After a meeting, use a quick ritual (e.g., reviewing your to-do list or taking a short walk) to help your brain switch back into work mode.\n\nThese strategies are tailored to your preference for protecting hyperfocus and minimizing the cognitive cost of interruptions. Would you like tips on how to negotiate meeting batching with your manager, or tools to automate reminders?", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0006-62-03234:3": 1.0, "clueweb22-en0030-54-15087:19": 0.5, "clueweb22-en0029-24-18238:8": 0.3333333333333333, "clueweb22-en0012-74-13153:6": 0.25, "clueweb22-en0018-75-17093:5": 0.2}, "ptkb_pr