{"metadata": {"team_id": "grilllab", "run_id": "grilllab-larf-finetuned-rankllm", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "8-1_10", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "When meetings interrupt your focus, the real challenge is the transition—getting your brain back into the task after being pulled away. Here are the most effective, ADHD-friendly strategies for managing these transitions in a sedentary office environment:\n\n**1. Pre-Meeting Reset**\n- Before a meeting, jot down exactly where you are in your current task (e.g., “Halfway through slide 3 of report”).\n- Leave a visible cue (sticky note, open document) so you know where to pick up.\n\n**2. Post-Meeting Ritual**\n- As soon as the meeting ends, take a brief physical reset: stand up, stretch, or walk for 1-2 minutes. This helps signal your brain to switch gears.\n- Review your pre-meeting note and spend 1-2 minutes re-reading your last work or notes before diving in. This minimizes “ramp-up” time and helps you re-engage faster.\n\n**3. Minimize Meeting Disruption**\n- If possible, schedule focused work blocks before and after meetings, so you’re not switching tasks too frequently.\n- Use a notebook or digital note to capture any ideas or to-dos that pop up during meetings, so they don’t distract you from returning to your main task.\n\n**4. Use Tools to Anchor Focus**\n- Consider fidget tools (stress ball, putty) or background music/white noise to help re-center after a meeting, especially in a quiet office.\n\nThese routines are designed for office workers with a sedentary lifestyle and help smooth the mental transition in and out of meetings. Would you like a sample template for a pre/post-meeting checklist, or tips on negotiating fewer unnecessary meetings?", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0038-88-03812:9": 1.0, "clueweb22-en0030-54-15087:19": 0.5, "clueweb22-en0025-75-16053:2": 0.3333333333333333, "clueweb22-en0032-22-09151:15": 0.25, "clueweb22-en0036-02-14625:4": 0.2}, "ptkb_provenance": ["I have a sedentary 