{"metadata": {"team_id": "grilllab", "run_id": "grilllab-larf-finetuned-rankllm", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "8-2_1", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "To get started with tattooing in Germany—especially since you already draw well and work in an office—here’s the most efficient, tailored path:\n\n**1. Build a Tattoo-Specific Portfolio**\n- Focus on tattoo designs, not just general art. Curate 10–20 pieces that show your versatility and understanding of tattoo styles.\n- This portfolio is essential for securing an apprenticeship.\n\n**2. Seek an Apprenticeship**\n- Apprenticeships are the industry standard and more valuable than formal degrees.\n- Look for certified tattoo artists or studios in your area willing to mentor you. Persistence is key, as competition is high.\n- If you can’t secure an apprenticeship immediately, consider working as a studio assistant (e.g., handling bookings). This can help you get your foot in the door.\n\n**3. Learn Hygiene and Business Practices**\n- During your apprenticeship, you’ll learn not just technique but also hygiene (critical for licensing in Germany) and business operations.\n- You may need to be vaccinated against hepatitis B.\n\n**4. Get Certified and Licensed**\n- After your apprenticeship, you’ll need to register with your local council for a tattoo license (Tätowiererlaubnis).\n- Both you and the studio must be licensed to operate legally.\n\n**Why This Fits You**\n- Your artistic skills give you a head start.\n- The apprenticeship route allows you to transition gradually from your office job, possibly starting part-time.\n- The process is structured and regulated in Germany, ensuring a clear path.\n\nWould you like tips on finding apprenticeship opportunities in your city or advice on building your tattoo portfolio?", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0028-52-08765:3": 1.0, "clueweb22-en0028-52-08765:4": 0.5, "clueweb22-en0025-87-09139:2": 0.3333333333333333, "clueweb22-en0025-87-09139:3": 0.25, "clueweb22-en0028-5