{"metadata": {"team_id": "grilllab", "run_id": "grilllab-larf-finetuned-rankllm", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "8-2_2", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "To get started with tattooing as a self-taught hobbyist, focus on three essentials: safe practice, the right equipment, and skill-building.\n\n**1. Safe Practice & Hygiene**\n- Always use antibacterial soap, disposable gloves, disinfectants, and antiseptic wipes.\n- Practice strict sterilization: clean your workspace and properly dispose of needles (use a sharps container).\n- Never tattoo on human skin until you’re confident in your skills and understand hygiene protocols.\n\n**2. Equipment for Beginners**\n- Starter tattoo kits are ideal—they include machines (for lining and shading), power supply, grips, needles, inks, and practice skin.\n    - **Recommended kits:** Hildbrandt (coil & rotary machines, comprehensive instructions), DragonHawk (two coil machines, inks, pre-sterilized needles, foot pedal, digital power supply).\n    - Pen-style machines (e.g., BRNOC Pen) are user-friendly for beginners and mimic the feel of drawing.\n- Kits often come with manuals and access to online tutorials—use these resources.\n\n**3. Skill-Building**\n- Begin on practice skin, fruit (like apples or pears), or synthetic alternatives.\n- Focus on line work, shading, and color packing.\n- Supplement hands-on practice with online courses or video tutorials for foundational techniques and safety.\n\n**Why this fits you:** This approach is flexible, affordable, and lets you learn at your own pace—ideal for a hobbyist with a busy schedule and a desire for hands-on creativity.\n\nWould you like specific kit recommendations available in Germany, or links to trusted online tutorials to get started?", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0024-21-00136:15": 1.0, "clueweb22-en0024-21-00136:17": 0.5, "clueweb22-en0042-99-10026:8": 0.3333333333333333, "clueweb22-en0024-22-17473:6": 0.25, "clueweb22-en0024-21-00136:14": 0.2}, "ptkb_provenance"