{"metadata": {"team_id": "grilllab", "run_id": "grilllab-larf-finetuned-rankllm", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "8-2_4", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "To get started with tattooing as a self-taught hobbyist, here’s what you need to know about tattoo needles—crucial for translating your drawing skills into skin art:\n\n**Tattoo Needle Types & Uses**\n- **Round Liner (RL):** For crisp, clean outlines. Ideal for line work and detail—perfect if you already draw well.\n- **Round Shader (RS):** For shading small areas and thicker lines.\n- **Flat Needles (F):** Used for straight, precise lines; good for geometric or script work.\n- **Magnum (M1/M2):** Best for shading and color packing over larger areas. The more needles grouped together, the softer and faster the shading.\n- **Shader Needles:** Specifically designed for smooth shading and gradients.\n\n**Needle Size Matters**\n- **Smaller groupings (e.g., 3RL):** Fine lines and detail.\n- **Larger groupings (e.g., 9M1):** Broad shading and color fills.\n\n**Cartridges vs. Traditional Needles**\n- **Cartridges:** Disposable, easy to swap, and more hygienic—great for beginners. Just ensure they fit your machine.\n- **Traditional Needles:** Require more setup and cleaning; less convenient for hobbyists.\n\n**Quality & Safety**\n- Always use medical-grade, sterile, single-use needles.\n- Choose reputable brands and check compatibility with your machine.\n\n**Next Steps**\n- Start with a variety pack of cartridges (liners and shaders) to experiment.\n- Practice different techniques on fake skin: outlining, shading, and color packing.\n\nWould you like recommendations for specific beginner-friendly needle sets or guidance on safe needle handling and hygiene?", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0022-31-15676:5": 1.0, "clueweb22-en0022-31-15676:4": 0.5, "clueweb22-en0046-30-10934:6": 0.3333333333333333, "clueweb22-en0015-60-03031:9": 0.25, "clueweb22-en0015-60-03031:8": 0.2}, "ptkb_provenance": ["I have a sedentary lifestyl