{"metadata": {"team_id": "grilllab", "run_id": "grilllab-larf-finetuned-rankllm", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "9-1_2", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "For mountain biking on forest trails and rough paths, saddle comfort is crucial. Here’s what matters most for your upgrade:\n\n**Best Saddle Types for Mountain Biking**\n- **Mountain Bike-Specific Saddles:** Designed with extra padding to absorb bumps and a durable cover to withstand rough terrain. They often have smoother, rounded edges to prevent snagging your shorts and may include scuff guards for crash protection.\n- **Shape & Padding:** Look for a saddle with enough padding to support your sit bones, but not so much that it interferes with pedaling. A slightly curved or “wavy” profile can help with comfort, but the right shape depends on your anatomy and riding style.\n- **Width:** The most common cause of discomfort is a saddle that doesn’t match your sit bone width. Consider measuring your sit bones or trying different widths at a bike shop.\n- **Nose Length:** Mountain bike saddles often have a shorter nose, allowing you to shift positions easily on climbs and technical sections.\n\n**Other Options**\n- **Comfort Saddles:** Wider and more padded, but may be too bulky for aggressive trail riding and can hinder efficient pedaling.\n- **Performance Saddles:** Lighter and narrower, but usually less padded—better for racing than trail comfort.\n\n**Personalized Recommendation**\nGiven your discomfort and trail use, prioritize a mountain bike-specific saddle with moderate padding, a durable cover, and the right width for your body.\n\nWould you like tips on measuring your sit bones or recommendations for specific saddle models?", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0007-52-02129:7": 1.0, "clueweb22-en0007-05-17998:10": 0.5, "clueweb22-en0007-52-02129:8": 0.3333333333333333, "clueweb22-en0044-50-07094:2": 0.25, "clueweb22-en0009-83-15326:2": 0.2}, "ptkb_provenance": ["I am not really handy with DIY.", "I recently