{"metadata": {"team_id": "grilllab", "run_id": "grilllab-larf-finetuned-rankllm", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "9-2_5", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "Great question—here’s what you need to know:\n\n**What is the jockey on a bike?**\n- The \"jockey\" refers to the two small wheels (also called jockey wheels or pulley wheels) inside your rear derailleur.\n- These wheels guide the chain as it moves between gears and keep the chain tensioned.\n    - The upper jockey wheel (guide pulley) sits closest to the cassette and helps shift the chain between gears.\n    - The lower jockey wheel (tension pulley) keeps the chain tight.\n\n**Why does this matter for your chain issue?**\n- If the jockey wheels are dirty, worn, or not spinning freely, they can cause the chain to jump off or behave unpredictably—even if the derailleur is aligned and the chain is the right length.\n- Worn jockey wheels can also make shifting noisy or rough.\n\n**What should you do next?**\n- Inspect both jockey wheels for wear (look for sharp, hooked, or chipped teeth).\n- Spin them by hand—if they feel gritty or don’t spin smoothly, they may need cleaning or replacement.\n- Clean them with a brush and degreaser, then re-lube.\n\nWould you like a quick step-by-step on how to check and clean your jockey wheels, or tips on replacing them if needed?", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0032-78-01787:1": 1.0, "clueweb22-en0039-65-07416:2": 0.5, "clueweb22-en0032-78-01787:0": 0.3333333333333333, "clueweb22-en0018-20-12295:16": 0.25, "clueweb22-en0039-65-07416:0": 0.2}, "ptkb_provenance": ["I'm a casual bike rider.", "I am not really handy with DIY.", "I like biking even in rainy weather and in the forest."]}], "references": {"clueweb22-en0032-78-01787:1": 1.0, "clueweb22-en0039-65-07416:2": 0.5, "clueweb22-en0032-78-01787:0": 0.3333333333333333, "clueweb22-en0018-20-12295:16": 0.25, "clueweb22-en0039-65-07416:0": 0.2, "clueweb22-en0018-20-12295:15": 0.16666666666666666, "clueweb22-en0040-35-09091:5": 0.