{"metadata": {"team_id": "grilllab", "run_id": "grilllab-larf-finetuned-rankllm", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "9-2_9", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "You asked if you can use car oil to lubricate your bike chain, and what the best option is.\n\n**Direct Answer:**  \nDo not use car oil (engine oil) on your bike chain. It is too thick, does not penetrate the chain well, and can attract dirt, causing more wear and reducing chain life.\n\n**Best Lubricant for Your Bike Chain:**\n- **Use a lubricant specifically designed for bicycles.** These are mineral-based oils or waxes formulated to:\n  - Penetrate chain links thoroughly\n  - Minimize dirt buildup (which accelerates wear)\n  - Provide adequate durability for cycling conditions\n- **Avoid:**  \n  - Car/motor oil (too thick, poor penetration, attracts dirt)\n  - Household spray lubricants (too thin, can dry out the chain)\n  - Vegetable oils (become gummy)\n  - WD-40 (good cleaner, not a lubricant)\n\n**Types of Bike Chain Lubes:**\n- **Wet lube:** Best for wet or muddy conditions; lasts longer but can attract more dirt.\n- **Dry lube or wax:** Cleaner, attracts less dirt, but needs more frequent application, especially in wet conditions.\n\n**How to Apply:**\n1. Clean the chain if dirty.\n2. Apply a small amount of bike-specific lube to each link.\n3. Let it soak in, then wipe off excess with a rag.\n\n**Why this matters:**  \nUsing the right lube keeps your chain running smoothly, extends its life, and prevents shifting issues.\n\nWould you like recommendations for specific brands, or tips on cleaning your chain before lubing?", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0024-04-10077:2": 1.0, "clueweb22-en0024-04-10077:3": 0.5, "clueweb22-en0019-98-16645:13": 0.3333333333333333, "clueweb22-en0002-63-04266:16": 0.25, "clueweb22-en0019-98-16645:14": 0.2}, "ptkb_provenance": ["I'm a casual bike rider.", "I sometimes get overwhelmed by too many technical choices.", "I am not really handy with DIY.", "I like biking even in