{"metadata": {"team_id": "grilllab", "run_id": "grilllab-larf-finetuned-rankllm", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "9-2_10", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "The best way to know when to re-lubricate your bike chain is to listen and look:\n\n**Key Signs Your Chain Needs Lubrication**\n- **Sound:** If your chain starts making noise—squeaking, rattling, or sounding rough—it’s time to lube. A well-lubricated chain should run quietly and shift smoothly.\n- **Appearance:** If the chain looks dry, rusty, or black with grime, it needs attention. A clean chain should have a slight sheen but not be sticky or coated in dirt.\n- **Feel:** If shifting becomes less smooth or the chain feels rough when you pedal, that’s another indicator.\n\n**How Often?**\n- There’s no fixed schedule; it depends on your riding conditions:\n  - **Wet/muddy rides:** Lube more often, possibly after every ride.\n  - **Dry, clean conditions:** Lube less frequently, but always after cleaning the chain.\n- Always wipe down your chain with a rag after each ride to remove excess lube and dirt—this prolongs chain life.\n\n**Why Not Car Oil?**\n- Bike-specific lubes are designed to resist water and attract less dirt. Car oil can gum up your drivetrain and require more frequent cleaning.\n\n**Quick Tip:** Apply lube to a clean, dry chain, wipe off the excess, and listen for noise as your main cue for reapplication.\n\nWould you like recommendations for specific chain lubes or tips on cleaning your chain efficiently?", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0028-70-13001:4": 1.0, "clueweb22-en0005-80-03758:10": 0.5, "clueweb22-en0005-80-03758:11": 0.3333333333333333, "clueweb22-en0028-70-13001:3": 0.25, "clueweb22-en0026-21-09546:1": 0.2}, "ptkb_provenance": ["I'm a casual bike rider.", "I sometimes get overwhelmed by too many technical choices.", "I am not really handy with DIY.", "I like biking even in rainy weather and in the forest."]}], "references": {"clueweb22-en0028-70-13001:4": 1.0, "clueweb22-en0005-80-0